---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const slopeCfg = {
  params: [
    { name: 'length', label: 'run length', min: 1, max: 20, step: 1, value: 10, unit: 'm' },
    { name: 'slope', label: 'slope', min: 1, max: 4, step: 0.5, value: 2, unit: '%' },
  ],
  readouts: [
    { label: 'vertical fall', expr: 'length*slope/100', unit: 'cm' },
    { label: 'within code? (1–4%)', expr: 'slope>=1&&slope<=4', unit: '' },
  ],
};

const tradeCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Two systems, one trade', note: 'Supply brings clean water in under pressure; drainage carries waste out by gravity. Different physics, different rules, same licence.' },
    { math: '2 · Health is the point', note: 'Plumbing codes exist because bad plumbing spreads cholera, typhoid and hepatitis. The plumber is a public health worker with a pipe wrench.' },
    { math: '3 · The kit', note: 'Pipe wrenches, cutters, torches, press tools, augers, inspection cameras — each pipe material (copper, PEX, PVC, cast iron) brings its own tools.' },
    { math: '4 · The hazards', note: 'Soldering torches, sewage exposure, trench collapse and cross-connections that could poison a building\'s drinking water — safety is trained, not assumed.' },
    { math: '5 · The code is the standard', note: 'The International Plumbing Code (and local amendments) decides pipe sizes, slopes, venting and backflow protection. Work is inspected against it.' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/plumbing/foundational/plumbing-tools-and-safety">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Plumbing is health engineering hidden in walls. Its two halves — pressurised supply and
    gravity drainage — obey different physics, and its history is literally the history of public
    health: the cities that learned to separate drinking water from sewage stopped dying of
    cholera. The modern trade is that separation, maintained to code.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="supply, drainage, hazards and the code" widget="equationStep" config={tradeCfg} />

  <h2>Drainage slope: the gentlest critical measurement</h2>
  <p>
    Waste lines rely on gravity, so slope is everything: too flat and solids settle; too steep
    and liquids outrun the solids, leaving blockages behind. The code's working band (about 1–4%
    for common drains) is narrow — run the calculator for a typical job and see how the fall
    accumulates over a basement run.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="slope, fall and code compliance over a run" widget="sliderLab" config={slopeCfg} />

  <h2>The hazards are professional-grade</h2>
  <p>
    Trenching deaths, sewage-borne infections, torch fires and scalding — plumbing safety modules
    are specific because the trade's hazards are. Add the invisible one: a cross-connection (a
    drop in supply pressure sucking waste into drinking water) can sicken a whole building, which
    is why backflow prevention devices exist and get tested.
  </p>

  <h2>Tools follow materials</h2>
  <p>
    Copper wants cutting, cleaning, flux and flame (or press fittings); PEX wants expanders and
    crimp rings; PVC wants primer and cement; cast iron wants no-hub couplings and respectful
    handling. Learning the trade is largely learning which tool, fitting and code section each
    material demands — and leaving the joints that pass inspection the first time.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> NCCER Plumbing curriculum; International Plumbing Code (ICC); PHCC educational materials.</p>
</TopicPage>
